From a9df690147a29c69c1b0f21b740cd3b711600e12 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SriAkhilSJ <206201543+SriAkhilSJ@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 13:43:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(tools): search_files reported an empty pattern on every
 miss, and taught nothing
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Run 6 of the preview batch harness sent three searches with regex escapes —

    search_files(path=., pattern=\.btn-primary)     ->  No results found for '' in '.'
    search_files(path=., pattern=btn-primary|\.btn) ->  No results found for '' in '.'
    search_files(path=., pattern=\.btn)             ->  No results found for '' in '.'

— then invented a class that exists nowhere (`btn-lg`) and spent the rest of the turn in the
terminal, timing out with no terminal frame. Two long-standing defects produced that, and the first
one is why the log is confusing to read:

* **the message named the wrong variable.** `No results found for '{query}'` — `query` is the legacy
  crusor alias, and it is empty whenever the caller sends `pattern`, which is what the schema
  requires. So every miss printed an empty pattern: it looked like the tool had dropped the
  argument, and the model's only recovery was to guess. It now names the pattern actually used.
* **the miss taught nothing.** This tool is a literal, case-insensitive substring match, so `\.btn`
  searches for a backslash that is not in any file — a wrong search that returns *empty*, not an
  error. The docstring said "literal substring match" and the model, under pressure, did not act on
  it. When the needle carries regex intent, de-escaped candidates are now checked in the same pass
  and the miss becomes a teaching error naming a pattern that does match, with a real example line
  (`'.btn'` matches, e.g. `styles.css:74: .btn-primary { … }`). Plain needles pay nothing: the
  candidate work only runs when the needle contains a backslash or `|`.

Also removed a dead scaffold comment ("Keep your EXISTING search logic below this point") left after
the function's return by an earlier auto-edit.

**The reordering theory is refuted and pinned.** The first reading of that log was that `path`-first
calls dropped the pattern. `_run_one` invokes `tool.invoke(args_dict)` and `coerce_tool_arg_aliases`
only renames aliases for *missing* keys, so key order cannot matter — measured: pattern-first and
path-first return identical output, hits and misses alike. The reordered calls in the log were simply
the escaped ones. `test_argument_order_is_irrelevant` encodes that so nobody reintroduces positional
handling on the strength of that log.

Ten pins in src/tests/test_search_files_literal_matching.py, driven through the real tool against the
lab fixture: the miss names the sent pattern (never an empty one), the three field-run patterns each
get the literal correction with a real example, a de-escaped form that matches nothing is *not*
suggested (honesty over helpfulness), a plain miss stays short, hits and the `search_code` alias are
unchanged, and the escaped form of a working search is still a miss. Verified to fail when either
defect is restored (3 failures each).

The harness also stops leaving strays behind. Run 6's agent backgrounded `python -m http.server` in
the workspace and it outlived the turn: `proc.kill()` reaps the direct child only. The harness now
spawns the engine in its own process group (POSIX) / process group on Windows and tears the whole
tree down, printing which was used (`engine stopped: process group (whole tree)`), and says what to
check when a turn ends without a terminal frame. Same technique as src/context/git_context.py, which
needed it for the same reason. The grandchild test spawns a real grandchild, reads its pid, kills the
tree and asserts the pid is gone — verified to fail against a direct-child kill. The Windows branch
(`taskkill /T /F`) cannot run in this sandbox and is honestly skipped there.

143 passed, 1 skipped. §7.5 of the runbook now explains both symptoms for the next run.

